DressCode
*********

.[perex]
DressCode is the dress code for your PHP code. It keeps your style clean, fixes most findings on the spot, and in the very same run can upgrade your code to modern PHP (from PHP 8.0 all the way to 8.6) as well as new versions of your libraries. Built on a lossless concrete syntax tree, it touches only what it needs to, and the rest of your file remains byte-for-byte identical. It wears no style of its own; instead, it dresses your code according to established standards: [PER Coding Style 3.1 |https://www.php-fig.org/per/coding-style/], PSR-12, Nette Coding Standard, or Symfony. And if you want anything done your own way, simply describe it key by key, exactly as it appears in your code, even differently for each part of your project.

For years, I relied on PHP CS Fixer and PHP_CodeSniffer, taking them for granted. Then came the day I needed to write a single custom rule. Only then did I realize how much work it takes when a tool sees code as nothing more than a flat array of tokens and has to guess its actual structure. DressCode was built so that you can write a new rule in an afternoon and trust what it fixes.


A Configuration That Describes Your Code
----------------------------------------

Code style tools often come with hundreds of rules, and DressCode is no exception, offering over two hundred built-in rules alone. But you never list them in your configuration. Instead, you simply describe how your code looks: the key identifies a specific construct in the code, and the value defines how it should be written.

```neon
use:
	- perCs
	- modernizations

braces:
	position:
		class: nextLine
spacing:
	concatenation: '$a.$b'
qualification:
	function:
		global: bare
		optimized: imported
```

You can read it right out loud: class opening braces go on the next line, concatenation dots have no surrounding spaces, standard global functions remain bare without a leading backslash, and compiler-optimized functions are imported. Expressing what those last two lines say isn't possible with any single setting in PHP CS Fixer; you have to piece it together through the interplay of two separate rules:

```php
'native_function_invocation' => [
	'include' => ['@compiler_optimized'],
	'scope' => 'namespaced',
	'strict' => true,
],
'global_namespace_import' => ['import_functions' => true],
```

The first rule adds a leading backslash to functions optimized by the compiler and strips it from the rest; the second rule then converts every backslash into an import statement. Over the exact same file, both tools arrive at the same code, except with PHP CS Fixer you have to derive the result yourself instead of reading a straightforward sentence. PHP_CodeSniffer with Slevomat can add a backslash to optimized functions or import all global functions across the board, but it cannot import only the optimized ones.

Yet you never have to write hundreds of configuration keys. The entire setup hinges on just two simple questions: **What should your code look like?** That is determined by the standard, which you pick by name. **And what else do you want from your code besides its appearance?** That's where [sets |configuration#Standards and Sets] come in, such as `modernizations` or `cleanup`. A set represents an intent rather than a list of keys: whenever DressCode learns a new modernization, the set delivers it without touching a single line of your configuration. Key by key, you then write only where your code differs from the standard.

You'll see the full decision path (such as `braces.position.class`) directly next to every finding in the terminal. You instantly know what to configure, what to suppress, or what to inspect in detail using `dresscode explain braces.position.class`. An overview of all sections and their keys is available on the [Rules |decisions/@home] page.


More Than How the Code Looks
----------------------------

Findings go well beyond whitespace and braces. Here are two files checked against decisions from the `classes`, `mistakes`, and `upgrading` sections, backed by [types from PHPStan |phpstan-types]:

/--pre .[terminal]
src/Settings.php
  risky  7:37  The parameter `$name` of `offsetGet()` must be named `$key`, as in `ArrayObject::offsetGet()`.  classes.overridingMethod.parameterName
               Risky because a call naming the argument `name:` stops working.

src/Users.php
  error  12:16  The `in_array()` call must pass `$strict = true`.                    mistakes.strictArgument
  risky  17:16  The `in_array()` call must pass `$strict = true`.                    mistakes.strictArgument
                Risky because a strict search no longer finds a value of another type.
  error  22:18  Method `Acme\Mail\Mailer::sendMessage()` is deprecated: use send().  upgrading.declarations.deprecatedMember
\--

The `Settings` class overrides `ArrayObject::offsetGet()` and names its parameter `$name`. While PHP allows this, a call like `$settings->offsetGet(key: 'theme')`, which works on every `ArrayObject`, will suddenly fail with `Unknown named parameter $key`. Renaming the parameter would break calls that already use `name:`. That makes it a risky fix (a fix that can alter runtime behavior), so the finding tells you exactly what is at risk.

Both `in_array()` calls produce the same message, but the fix treats them differently. On line 12, type analysis confirms that an `int` is searched for in a `list<int>`, so `fix` adds `true` on its own. On line 17, the value searched for is `mixed`, and the fix waits until you [explicitly permit it |configuration#risky-fixes]. The library marked the method `sendMessage()` with `@deprecated use send()`, and `fix` rewrites the call to `send()` right away.


Not Just Style: Upgrading Code Too
----------------------------------

A tool that truly understands code doesn't have to stop at whitespace and braces. As soon as you bump your minimum PHP version in `composer.json`, the `modernizations` set automatically rewrites your code to take advantage of new language features, and DressCode formats the result according to your chosen standard in the very same run:

```php .[before]
return trim(strtolower(strip_tags($title)));
```

```php .[after]
return $title |> strip_tags(...) |> strtolower(...) |> trim(...);
```

Upgrading third-party libraries works just as smoothly (see [Upgrading Code |upgrade]): bump dependency versions in `composer.json`, run `dresscode upgrade today`, and your code is rewritten to the new API required by the updated packages, with zero configuration and without disrupting your existing code style:

```php .[before]
$image->resize(300, 200, Image::FIT | Image::SHRINK_ONLY);
file_put_contents($this->file, Json::encode($products, Json::PRETTY));
```

```php .[after]
$image->resize(300, 200, Image::OrSmaller | Image::ShrinkOnly);
file_put_contents($this->file, Json::encode($products, pretty: true));
```

For Nette, the `dresscode/upgrade-nette` package provides migrations across all libraries (mostly from version 3.0 up to the latest release): handling renamed classes, constants, methods, and parameters. Dedicated upgrade packages are also available for Symfony, Laravel, Dibi, and Texy. DressCode consults PHPStan for precise type analysis, ensuring deprecated APIs are rewritten based on what a variable actually is. Formatting and version upgrades are handled by a single tool, with one unified configuration and a clean, predictable diff.


Coverage of PER Coding Style and PSR-12
---------------------------------------

Claiming standard compliance is easy. Putting that claim to the test against the competition is what actually counts. I took the Laravel framework codebase, formatted it using the `perCs` preset, and handed it over to PHP CS Fixer with its official PER Coding Style rule set. Across 1,696 files, it found only 9 spots to change. Conversely, running DressCode on Laravel after PHP CS Fixer revealed 1,201 places where PER Coding Style mandates something different (such as strictly one item per line or trailing commas in multiline lists). Testing PSR-12 against `phpcbf` yielded similar results: DressCode discovered 772 lingering violations, including missing constant visibility, which `phpcbf` reports, but cannot fix on its own. Detailed numbers for Symfony and what the other tools still find after DressCode can be found on the [How DressCode Works |how-it-works#PER Coding Style and PSR-12 Compared to PHP CS Fixer and PHP_CodeSniffer] page.


Speed Compared to PHP CS Fixer and PHP_CodeSniffer
--------------------------------------------------

DressCode is engineered to run after every edit of a file. In that workflow, single-file latency is what matters most: DressCode takes roughly one-third to one-half the time of PHP CS Fixer (118 ms vs. 323 ms on a small file). It is noticeably faster across entire projects as well when performing equivalent work. With decisions matching PHP CS Fixer's PER Coding Style set, it requires only 64% to 90% of PHP CS Fixer's time in a single process and 46% to 64% in parallel runs across the codebases of Laravel, WordPress, Symfony, and Nette. Symfony, checked using its own PHP CS Fixer configuration (converted via `migrate config`), completes in 56% of the original time (14.2 s vs. 25.3 s), even though PHP CS Fixer additionally runs over a hundred phpDoc and PHPUnit rules that DressCode does not touch.

The full `perCs` preset does far more than that set: it automatically wraps long signatures, complex conditions, call chains, and arrays onto separate lines, while ensuring correct casing for identifiers. In a direct preset-to-preset comparison, it finishes Laravel in 72% of PHP CS Fixer's time and WordPress in 118%, where the extra decisions have the greatest volume of work to do.

Compared to PHP_CodeSniffer, the speed advantage is especially apparent during fixes: `dresscode fix` needs only 36% to 54% of the time `phpcbf` spends on PSR-12. Plain reporting without fixing is faster in `phpcs`, simply because `dresscode check` computes the necessary fixes in memory during inspection. Furthermore, upgrading to a newer PHP takes a fraction of the time Rector requires: on Symfony 3.4, it completes in 1.6 s compared to Rector's 128.5 s. Even with [types from PHPStan |phpstan-types] enabled, DressCode remains significantly faster: on Laravel, it finishes in 4.4 s vs. 10.5 s with a warm PHPStan cache (and 9.0 s vs. 195 s from a cold cache).

Measured in October 2026 on PHP 8.5 against PHP CS Fixer 3.95, PHP_CodeSniffer 4.0, and Rector 2.6 (median of three runs).


